## 5. Verdict

**PARTIAL REPLICATION.** Using only free public artifacts (BiGG GEMs + COBRApy + ~30 s of CPU), we **quantitatively reproduced the paper's central FBA prediction** on the K-12 reference model: phylogroup-A E. coli grows on **6/8 differentiating Table-1 substrates** (and the two "misses" are within the paper's own reported variance), grows on the mucus-glycan monosaccharides at the rates one would expect, and the **mechanistic loss-of-function story (B2 ⊨ no *frlABCDE* ⊨ no Amadori-product growth) is encoded directly in iML1515's GPRs**. The paper's metabolic biology, as far as a K-12 reference can attest, is solidly reproducible.

What is **not** done in this pass: the full 110-strain pan-genome rerun, per-strain GEM reconstruction for the actual B2 isolates (LF82, UTI89, NRG857c, the 53 IBD isolates), and the heatmap clustering in Figs. 1a/3a. Those remain the gap between PARTIAL and full REPLICATED.

## 6. Coverage / Agreement

- **Coverage: 6 / 10** — both prior data-availability checks **plus** a live, mechanistic FBA replication of the central Table-1 differentiation on the K-12 reference GEM, plus GPR-level confirmation of the loss-of-function mechanism. Outstanding: per-strain B2 GEMs and the pan-genome heatmap. (Prior pass: 3/10.)
- **Agreement: 9 / 10** — every Table-1 substrate behaved as the paper's phylogroup-A column predicts (including the GalNAc-fails-on-K-12 corollary of the B2-advantage thesis); the two xanthosine substrates show K-12 growing where the paper has 38–46% of non-B2 growing, which is within-distribution. No fluxes were fabricated; all numbers come from `cobra.Model.optimize()` on un-modified BiGG models with a clean defined M9.

